From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ilya Vysotsky <il.mohoff2016@yandex.ru>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 23:48:04 +0400
Subject: [PATCH] Memory pressure on Windows 10 Mobile: fresh collections
 rationed by cost and benefit, the address space checked cheaply

Build 172 re-sent a fresh "low-memory" every 30 s while pressure was
serious. In a single process that is a full, non-incremental, shrinking GC,
a cycle collection and usually a second GC inside the notification, on the
one main thread. On a Lumia 650 with Discord seven of them took 1.0, 2.4,
1.7, 5.4, 6.9, 12.2 and 21.9 s; only one freed much. Its address-space
watcher walked 1668 regions (49 ms) every second.

Now:
- pressure comes on with one fresh "low-memory" and ends with
  memory-pressure-stop only after 30 s of nothing wanting it, so a phone
  crossing the Low/Medium line every few seconds stays in one spell;
- while it is serious (the phone at High or over its limit, or a failed
  allocation) a cheap clock sends "low-memory-ongoing" every
  pressure-rearm-ms, backing off to twice and four times that while the
  purge frees under 4 MB of commit;
- a fresh re-arm is sent only max(30 s, 9 x the last one's cost) after the
  last, when its predicted cost (the last cost scaled by JS heap growth) is
  at most 3 s, and not after two in a row that freed too little. What one
  freed is the process's commit (PrivateUsage) before, right after, and
  after the jemalloc purge that follows;
- what the phone asks for is throttled to one cache purge per 10, 20, 40 s;
- the address space going low is an edge with a 30 s hold, not a state: no
  collection in 172 made the largest free piece any bigger;
- the watcher checks the 8 largest free pieces every second (at most 3
  VirtualQuery calls each) and walks the whole space only when that cannot
  tell, at most once per 100 times a walk's cost;
- going to the background is marked at once (gecko_w10m_app_state), the
  clocks start again on the way back, and a collection that ran across a
  suspension is not timed;
- notes: 'mem: let go #N' with cost, prediction, JS heap and commit before
  and after, 'fresh re-arm skipped', a summary of each spell with the share
  of main-thread time it took, and 'vm watch: last 120 s'.

The shell now passes every change of level, up and down.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
